      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Run `nvidia-smi` \u2014 that's the direct way. On this machine it shows a single GPU:\n\n```\nindex=0, Tesla T4, 15360 MiB, currently 0 MiB used, uuid=GPU-8c26e74b-...\n```\n\nSince there's only one GPU visible on this container, `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES = \"0\"` (what's already in the notebook) is correct here \u2014 there's nothing else to pick between.\n\nA couple of notes:\n- Your shell's `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` is currently unset (empty), so this container isn't pre-restricting you \u2014 the notebook's own `os.environ[\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"] = \"0\"` line is what pins it.\n- If your team's setup has one JupyterHub container per person (each with its own single GPU, like this one), there's no real \"assignment\" to check beyond `nvidia-smi` showing what's there. If instead you're on a shared multi-GPU box, run `nvidia-smi` and look at `memory.used`/running processes per index to see which ones are already busy, and check with whoever set up the workshop for which index is yours \u2014 that assignment isn't visible from environment variables alone."
    },
